Core Protocols
DHCP — Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
DHCP automatically assigns network configuration to devices joining a network. It provides:

IP address

Subnet mask

Default gateway

DNS server

DHCP uses a four‑step process known as DORA:

Discover

Offer

Request

Acknowledge

DHCP prevents manual configuration errors and avoids IP address conflicts by dynamically assigning addresses.

ARP — Address Resolution Protocol
ARP maps an IPv4 address to a MAC address so devices on the same Ethernet or WiFi network can communicate.

ARP Request is broadcast on the local network.

ARP Reply contains the target device’s MAC address.

This allows hosts to build proper Layer 2 frames for communication.

NAT — Network Address Translation
NAT allows private IP addresses to communicate on the public Internet by translating them to a router’s public IP. It conserves IPv4 address space and adds a layer of abstraction between internal and external networks.

ICMP — Internet Control Message Protocol
ICMP is used for diagnostics and error reporting. Two major tools rely on it:

Ping
Ping sends ICMP Echo Requests and receives Echo Replies. It measures round‑trip time (RTT) and packet loss, helping determine whether a target is reachable.

Traceroute / Tracert
Traceroute uses the Time‑to‑Live (TTL) field in IP headers. Each router decreases the TTL, and when it reaches zero, the router sends an ICMP Time Exceeded message. This reveals each hop along the path to a destination.

Routing Protocols
Routing protocols determine the best path for data across networks.

OSPF — Open Shortest Path First
Link‑state protocol

Routers share topology information

Builds a complete network map

Calculates shortest paths using Dijkstra’s algorithm

EIGRP — Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol
Cisco proprietary

Hybrid algorithm

Considers metrics like bandwidth and delay

Fast convergence and efficient routing

BGP — Border Gateway Protocol
Core routing protocol of the global Internet

Connects autonomous systems (ISPs, large organizations)

Determines inter‑network paths worldwide

RIP — Routing Information Protocol
Distance‑vector protocol

Chooses routes based on hop count

Simple and used in small networks

